      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The Time Value of Money (TVM) is the single most fundamental concept in all of finance. Every calculation in this unit — present value, future value, valuation of assets, opportunity cost — is built on this one idea: a sum of money available today is worth more than the identical sum available in the future. This is not an opinion or a cultural convention. It is a mathematical and economic reality that applies universally — in Tashkent, Tokyo, or New York. Once you truly understand the time value of money, you will never look at financial decisions the same way again. The Three Reasons Money Has a Time Value There are exactly three reasons why money today is worth more than money tomorrow. All three are real and all three operate simultaneously in every financial market. • Reason 1: Investment Opportunity: Money you hold today can be put to work immediately. You can deposit it in a bank, buy assets, start a business, or invest in stocks. Each of these generates a return. A sum that sits idle in the future produces nothing today. This is the most important reason — money has productive potential, and time is required to realize it. • Reason 2: Inflation: In virtually every economy, prices rise over time. In Uzbekistan, inflation averaged 8–12% annually in recent years. This means 1,000,000 UZS today buys more goods and services than 1,000,000 UZS will in five years. The purchasing power of money erodes continuously. Holding uninvested cash is therefore not neutral — it is a guaranteed loss in real terms. • Reason 3: Risk and Uncertainty: The future is uncertain. A promise of money in the future carries the risk that it may never arrive: the borrower may default, the investment may fail, political or economic events may intervene. Money held today is certain. Money promised in the future is not. Rational people require compensation for this uncertainty — which is why future money is discounted."
